+/* 16-bit integer multiply for PDP-11 targets without the EIS MUL
+   instruction (e.g. -m10 / KP1801VM1).  On such CPUs GCC emits a
+   __mulhi3 libcall for any HImode multiply it cannot open-code, but the
+   generic libgcc does not provide one, so a bare link fails with
+   "undefined reference to __mulhi3".
+
+   This is written in assembly on purpose: a shift-and-add loop expressed
+   in C is recognised by the optimiser (at -Os, which libgcc uses) as a
+   multiplication and rewritten into a __mulhi3 call -- i.e. the routine
+   would recurse infinitely into itself.  Hand assembly is immune to that
+   idiom recognition (the same reason libgcc's mem* and other primitives
+   are, or are built, this way).
+
+   ABI: arguments and result are HImode.  The product's low 16 bits are
+   identical for signed and unsigned operands, so this single routine
+   serves both the smul and umul optabs.  r0/r1 are call-clobbered
+   scratch; r2 is call-saved and therefore preserved.  Result in r0.
